tep from disaster, while another in the US has gen Z revelling in Chinese lifestyle hacksAcross two online worlds that are normally splintered, over the last few months there has been a mirroring of sorts. On TikTok and Instagram, young people are diving into the joys of Chinese culture – from drinking hot water to playing mahjong – all under the banner of “Chinamaxxing”. On the Chinese internet, however, the US is losing its decades-long grip on soft power, and is instead being replaced by a darker trend: the kill line.The kill line is a dangerous place to be. In gaming, the term refers to the point at which a player’s strength is so depleted that one more blow could lead to total wipeout. In China, the term refers to the risks that come with daily life in the US. Continue reading","fullContent":"In China, one social media trend hangs on the idea that a life in the US is always one step from disaster, while another in the US has gen Z revelling in Chinese lifestyle hacksAcross two online worlds that are normally splintered, over the last few months there has been a mirroring of sorts. On TikTok and Instagram, young people are diving into the joys of Chinese culture – from drinking hot water to playing mahjong – all under the banner of “Chinamaxxing”. On the Chinese internet, however, the US is losing its decades-long grip on soft power, and is instead being replaced by a darker trend: the kill line.The kill line is a dangerous place to be. In gaming, the term refers to the point at which a player’s strength is so depleted that one more blow could lead to total wipeout. In China, the term refers to the risks that come with daily life in the US. Continue reading","link":"https://www.theguardian.com/world/ng-interactive/2026/mar/13/chinamaxxing-social-media-trend-gen-z-china-us","sourceId":"guardian","sourceName":"The Guardian","sourceCategory":"News","sourceColor":"#DC2626","sourceIcon":"newspaper","pubDate":"2026-03-13T02:42:20.000Z","author":"Amy Hawkins in Beijing","imageUrl":"https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/77631e74cc0516d0682cff1b4149d02818843eda/0_0_4965_3972/master/4965.jpg?width=140&amp;quality=85&amp;auto=format&amp;fit=max&amp;s=b52943e01f3acf9ddd483f2889efb323"},{"id":1207991850,"title":"We don’t know if AI-powered toys are safe, but they’re here anyway","description":"Toys powered by AI show a worrying lack of emotional understanding. But we need to understand the risks and benefits of the technology so the industry can be regulated, not outright banned","fullContent":"Toys powered by AI show a worrying lack of emotional understanding. But we need to understand the risks and benefits of the technology so the industry can be regulated, not outright banned","link":"https://www.newscientist.com/article/2518741-we-dont-know-if-ai-powered-toys-are-safe-but-theyre-here-anyway/?utm_campaign=RSS%7CNSNS&#038;utm_source=NSNS&#038;utm_medium=RSS&#038;utm_content=home","sourceId":"newscientist","sourceName":"New Scientist","sourceCategory":"Science","sourceColor":"#0891B2","sourceIcon":"biotech","pubDate":"2026-03-13T00:01:57.000Z","author":null,"imageUrl":null},{"id":15064890,"title":"A young girl is knocked over at Tokyo crossing – what’s behind Japan’s ‘bumping’ trend?","description":"Viral video of girl being shoved by fellow pedestrian has reignited debate over butsukari – with experts blaming stress and gender dynamicsIt starts out as a heartwarming clip. A young girl, clearly delighted to be in Tokyo, beams as she makes a peace sign to the camera. Seconds later, she is shoved to the ground from behind by a woman wearing a surgical mask. The assailant doesn’t skip a beat, striding out of shot of the clip filmed by the girl’s mother.This was no accidental clash of shoulders in a crowded place, but one of the most visible examples of a spate of butsukari otoko – “bumping man” – shoving incidents in Japan that experts attribute to a combination of gender dynamics and the stresses of modern life. Continue reading","fullContent":"Viral video of girl being shoved by fellow pedestrian has reignited debate over butsukari – with experts blaming stress and gender dynamicsIt starts out as a heartwarming clip. A young girl, clearly delighted to be in Tokyo, beams as she makes a peace sign to the camera. Seconds later, she is shoved to the ground from behind by a woman wearing a surgical mask. The assailant doesn’t skip a beat, striding out of shot of the clip filmed by the girl’s mother.This was no accidental clash of shoulders in a crowded place, but one of the most visible examples of a spate of butsukari otoko – “bumping man” – shoving incidents in Japan that experts attribute to a combination of gender dynamics and the stresses of modern life. Continue reading","link":"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/12/japan-butsukari-otoko-bumping-man-trend-explained-tokyo-girl-shoved","sourceId":"guardian","sourceName":"The Guardian","sourceCategory":"News","sourceColor":"#DC2626","sourceIcon":"newspaper","pubDate":"2026-03-12T05:00:11.000Z","author":"Justin McCurry in Tokyo and Tania Lee","imageUrl":"https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/bafe6ee30fcd1954e5dd910b112c79fd770295d6/381_0_5000_4000/master/5000.jpg?width=140&amp;quality=85&amp;auto=format&amp;fit=max&amp;s=dc931b10d6e82d3945ef693619b4f39e"},{"id":2138046319,"title":"Why are we so obsessed with protein? A new book looks for answers","description":"Samantha King and Gavin Weedon's new book Protein digs deep into the nutrient's role in our health. But can it tell you how much you should be eating? Alexandra Thompson explores","fullContent":"Samantha King and Gavin Weedon's new book Protein digs deep into the nutrient's role in our health. But can it tell you how much you should be eating? Alexandra Thompson explores","link":"https://www.newscientist.com/article/mg26935863-900-why-are-we-so-obsessed-with-protein-a-new-book-looks-for-answers/?utm_campaign=RSS%7CNSNS&#038;utm_source=NSNS&#038;utm_medium=RSS&#038;utm_content=home","sourceId":"new